**New evidence**
- The UN said Turkey was a genuinely useful host for Russia-Ukraine talks in April 2022, which matters for 2B.1 and 2B.7 because it shows Erdoğan’s government was used as a mediation venue rather than only as a coercive actor.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN Relief Chief: Turkey presented itself as a genuinely valuable and useful host","publisher":"United Nations in Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://turkiye.un.org/en/177959-un-relief-chief-turkey-presented-itself-genuinely-valuable-and-useful-host","claim":"Martin Griffiths said Turkey had presented itself to Russia and Ukraine as a 'genuinely valuable and useful host' for talks, and that the Turks came closest to mediation among member states.","locator":"L59-L63","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UN commentary from a senior official on Turkey's mediation role in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous with the Ukraine war diplomacy in spring 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Supports attribution to Erdoğan's government as the host and facilitator of talks; this is about venue and facilitation, not sole control of outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["This is a favorable UN assessment, not an independent audit of effectiveness.","It does not show that the mediation changed the war's overall trajectory."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- The Black Sea Grain Initiative was launched in Istanbul by Russia, Türkiye, Ukraine, and the UN, and its stated purpose was to move grain safely and reduce global hunger. This is one of the clearest 2B.1 and 2B.7 positive items.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Black Sea Grain Initiative Frequently Asked Questions","publisher":"United Nations in Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://turkiye.un.org/en/195522-black-sea-grain-initiative-frequently-asked-questions","claim":"The Black Sea Grain Initiative was launched in Istanbul by the Russian Federation, Türkiye, Ukraine and the United Nations on 22 July 2022 to enable safe transport of grain and reduce global hunger and food insecurity.","locator":"L60-L63","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN explanatory page on the initiative's creation, purpose, and structure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdoğan's government as host and participant, but implementation was multilateral.","contrary_evidence":["This is a humanitarian and logistical initiative, not a treaty settlement or ceasefire.","It shows mediation capacity more than broad peace leadership across all conflicts."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- The UN later said the Istanbul center enabled safe transport of Ukrainian grain and thanked Türkiye for providing the platform. This strengthens the case that Erdoğan’s government helped operationalize a peaceful arrangement.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN welcomes new centre to put Ukraine grain exports deal into motion","publisher":"United Nations in Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://turkiye.un.org/en/192587-un-welcomes-new-centre-put-ukraine-grain-exports-deal-motion","claim":"The Joint Coordination Centre in Istanbul would enable safe transportation of Ukrainian grain, and the Secretary-General thanked Türkiye for providing the platform that helped operationalize the initiative.","locator":"L59-L63, L73-L76","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UN report on the operational start of the grain corridor and Türkiye's hosting role.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous with July 2022 implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Supports Erdoğan's government as facilitator and host of the arrangement.","contrary_evidence":["The center was multinational and UN-brokered, so credit is shared.","This is not evidence of broader restraint in military affairs."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- The Turkish government itself said the grain agreement was coordinated from Ankara and later extended for 120 days. Useful as ruler-linked attribution, but it is self-reported and favorable.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye’s role in extending the Black Sea Grain Initiative receives high praise","publisher":"Republic of Türkiye Directorate of Communications","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.iletisim.gov.tr/english/haberler/detay/turkiyes-role-in-extending-the-black-sea-grain-initiative-receives-high-praise","claim":"The grain shipment agreement was carried out under the coordination of Ankara under the auspices of President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, and Erdoğan noted the initiative had been extended for 120 days beginning November 19, 2022.","locator":"L53-L55","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct state communication about Turkey's role and the extension, but the framing is favorable and self-reported.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about a 2022 extension decision.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes the coordination role to Erdoğan's government.","contrary_evidence":["Self-published government material may overstate Turkey's unilateral influence.","The extension still depended on quadrilateral agreement."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

- NATO’s Madrid memorandum matters because Erdoğan chose bargaining and compromise over rupture. It also shows he used alliance leverage to pursue what Ankara described as security concerns, not a nuclear or interstate war escalation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye, Finland, and Sweden sign agreement paving the way for Finnish and Swedish NATO membership","publisher":"NATO","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.nato.int/en/news-and-events/articles/news/2022/06/28/turkiye-finland-and-sweden-sign-agreement-paving-the-way-for-finnish-and-swedish-nato-membership","claim":"Erdoğan, Niinistö and Andersson agreed a trilateral memorandum addressing Türkiye's legitimate security concerns, paving the way for Finland and Sweden's NATO membership.","locator":"L813-L815","source_type":"official/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary NATO statement on the agreement and its diplomatic significance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Erdoğan as a participating head of government in the negotiations.","contrary_evidence":["This is alliance bargaining, not a pure peace settlement.","Turkey used veto leverage, so the move was cooperative but also coercive."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Reuters independently described Turkey lifting its veto after the three sides agreed to protect each other's security. This is a useful external confirmation of the NATO bargain.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey lifts veto on Finland, Sweden joining NATO, clearing path for expansion","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/us-to-press-turkey-as-finland-sweden-hope-for-nato-breakthrough-2841742","claim":"Turkey lifted its veto over Finland and Sweden's bid to join NATO after the three nations agreed to protect each other's security, ending a weeks-long drama that tested allied unity against Russia's invasion of Ukraine.","locator":"L433-L440","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is generally strong on fast-moving diplomacy and this piece is independently aligned with NATO's own account.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the June 2022 negotiation.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdoğan's negotiating posture, though the breakthrough was collective.","contrary_evidence":["The article also emphasizes the dramatic pressure and strategic bargaining involved.","It does not show underlying peace preferences beyond this deal."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Zelenskyy’s office said Erdoğan personally played a role in the prisoner-exchange arrangements and provided guarantees for Ukrainians staying in Türkiye. That is strong 2B.7 evidence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Volodymyr Zelenskyy had a telephone conversation with Recep Tayyip Erdoğan","publisher":"Official website of the President of Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.president.gov.ua/en/news/volodimir-zelenskij-proviv-telefonnu-rozmovu-z-redzhepom-tay-78077","claim":"The President of Ukraine highly appreciated the personal role of Recep Tayyip Erdoğan in organizing the recent exchange of prisoners of war and said Erdoğan expressed readiness to contribute to international efforts to prevent the nuclear threat.","locator":"L205-L210","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct state-level confirmation from Ukraine's presidential office of Erdoğan's role in the exchange and follow-on diplomacy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous with September 2022 diplomacy.","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Erdoğan, though the exchange itself was negotiated multilaterally.","contrary_evidence":["The source is Ukrainian and therefore naturally favorable to Turkey's mediation.","It concerns prisoner release and ZNPP diplomacy, not a wider peace settlement."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Ukraine's presidential office also said the Mariupol defender release required negotiations at several levels and that Erdoğan personally participated and gave guarantees for safe stay in Türkiye. This is one of the clearest 2B.7 items.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The success of the operation to release people from Russian captivity was facilitated by the active involvement of international partners of Ukraine - Head of the Office of the President","publisher":"Official website of the President of Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://www.president.gov.ua/en/news/uspihu-operaciyi-zi-zvilnennya-lyudej-iz-rosijskogo-polonu-s-77945","claim":"Andriy Yermak said Türkiye played an important role in the Mariupol release process, that the Presidents of Ukraine and Türkiye personally reached important agreements, and that Erdoğan provided guarantees for the safe stay of the released citizens in Türkiye.","locator":"L202-L208","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official attribution from Ukraine's presidential office, with named participants and negotiation details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the September 2022 prisoner release.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributes personal participation to Erdoğan, while still acknowledging multilateral negotiation.","contrary_evidence":["This is a Ukrainian official account and therefore favorable to Turkey.","It shows facilitation of a humanitarian exchange, not conflict termination."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

- OHCHR documented a 2022 communication alleging indiscriminate Turkish airstrikes in civilian areas of northern Iraq that killed and injured civilians and damaged civilian facilities. This is important negative evidence for 2B.4, 2B.5, 2B.6, and 2B.9.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Communication report and search","publisher":"OHCHR / UN Special Procedures","publication_date":"2022-07-20","url":"https://spcommreports.ohchr.org/TmSearch/SearchCode?code=SYR+1%2F2019%3B+TUR+5%2F2022","claim":"Information received concerned alleged indiscriminate airstrikes by the Turkish armed forces against the Kurdistan Workers Party in civilian areas in northern Iraq, resulting in the death and injury of civilians and damage to civilian facilities.","locator":"L30-L40","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary OHCHR special-procedure communication record, though it records allegations rather than a judicial finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Turkish armed forces under Erdoğan's government; operational orders are not spelled out in the communication.","contrary_evidence":["This is an allegation record, not a legal finding.","Turkey would frame the strikes as counterterrorism and self-defense."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.6","2B.9","2B.10"]}

- HRW’s December 2022 report says Turkish strikes in northeast Syria damaged populated areas and critical infrastructure, with at least 10 civilian deaths reported. This is strong contrary evidence for proportionality and civilian protection.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Northeast Syria: Turkish Strikes Exacerbate Humanitarian Crisis","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/12/07/northeast-syria-turkish-strikes-exacerbate-humanitarian-crisis","claim":"Human Rights Watch said Turkish airstrikes since November 20, 2022 were damaging populated areas and critical infrastructure across north and northeast Syria, and that at least 10 civilians were reported to have died.","locator":"L612-L614","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"HRW combines field reporting, local interviews, and specific event descriptions in a contemporary rights-focused assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about late-2022 operations.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Turkish state and its military campaign under Erdoğan, though tactical orders were issued through defense channels.","contrary_evidence":["Turkey said the campaign targeted militants and was retaliation for the Istanbul bombing.","The report is rights-focused and not a complete military-history account."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.5","2B.6","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 25 to 40 documents across UN, NATO, Reuters, Turkish official communications, Ukraine official channels, OHCHR, and HRW.
- Kept sources that added something new on one of four things: Erdoğan’s actual diplomatic choices, his role in peace-brokering, civilian-risk criticism of Turkish operations, or formal authority/context.
- Rejected or de-emphasized generic NATO background pages, duplicate Reuters syndications of the same story, broad Turkey background pages, and older war-crime reports that were useful only as pre-2022 context.
- Existing 2B-relevant evidence from the previous thread was reused rather than recopied, especially the Turkish cross-border operation coverage and the NATO memorandum material.

**Remaining gaps**
- I still do not have a direct 2022 source showing Erdoğan personally weighed and rejected a specific peaceful alternative for the Syria/Iraq operation, beyond the official anti-terror justification and the contrary rights reporting.
- The record is stronger on mediation than on peace settlement. The grain corridor and prisoner exchanges are real achievements, but they are not the same as ending a major conflict.
- More local-language archival reporting could sharpen 2B.3 and 2B.6, especially on the rationale for the November 2022 operation and whether civilian-risk warnings were internalized.
- More independent investigation of the Iraq/Syria strikes would help 2B.4 and 2B.9, especially on command responsibility and civilian casualty verification.
